detail.abstract:
Purpose: This study investigates the transmission mechanisms linking social expenditures to sustainable development in Germany (1995–2025) using 372 monthly observations. It examines the mediating role of the Human Development Index (HDI) and the moderating influence of financial market depth (SMD).&nbsp;
<div>
 Design/Methodology/Approach: A dual-layered mediation–moderation framework is estimated on 372 monthly observations using OLS with Newey–West HAC standard errors (lag = 12). Indirect effects are validated via bias-corrected and accelerated (BCa) bootstrapping with 5,000 replications. Simple slope analysis at ±1 SD of the moderator confirms moderation. Dynamic LDV and Granger-causality robustness checks are included. All interactions are constructed from mean-centred variables following Hayes (2022) and Aiken and West (1991).&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Findings: HDI is the dominant driver of sustainability (β = 169.52, p &lt; 0.001). Health expenditure positively and significantly drives HDI (α₂ = 0.0239, p &lt; 0.001) with a significant positive mediated path (BCa 95% CI: [3.408, 4.755]). Education expenditure's HDI impact is thresholdconditional on SMD (~45–50% of GDP). Both moderation effects in the SDS equation are significant at monthly frequency.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Originality/Value: This study provides high-frequency monthly evidence (N = 372) that financial structures asymmetrically govern the social returns of education and health spending, advancing beyond linear fiscal models for advanced economies. Future Scope: Future research should extend to multi-country panels incorporating governance and environmental policy variables.
</div>
